WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:02.600
Now think about stored procedures like this.

00:02.600 --> 00:08.240
Every time you go to a coffee shop, you say, I would like a large coffee with a coconut milk, no

00:08.240 --> 00:10.080
sugar and extra whipped cream.

00:10.280 --> 00:12.200
And you repeat this over and over.

00:12.240 --> 00:16.720
Each time you go to this coffee shop and now you are working with stored procedures, it's going to

00:16.720 --> 00:17.440
be like this.

00:17.480 --> 00:23.240
Whenever you go to the coffee shop, you just say, give me my usual and the barista know exactly what

00:23.240 --> 00:28.400
you mean behind that, and you will get exactly your order without specifying and repeating everything

00:28.440 --> 00:29.520
word by word.

00:29.520 --> 00:32.760
And this is exactly what's going to happen if you work with stored procedures.

00:32.920 --> 00:33.960
So let's have some coffee.

00:33.960 --> 00:34.400
Right.

00:36.680 --> 00:37.160
All right.

00:37.160 --> 00:38.240
So now we can continue.

00:38.280 --> 00:38.600
All right.

00:38.600 --> 00:40.400
So now let's start again from the scratch.

00:40.400 --> 00:41.840
We have always these two sides.

00:41.840 --> 00:44.760
We have the client side and the server side of the database.

00:44.760 --> 00:46.800
And what we have learned we have like a database.

00:46.800 --> 00:52.840
And you as a user you can go and create like different SQL statements like for example, you can create

00:52.840 --> 00:59.360
like an SQL select statements in order to retrieve data from the database or another SQL statement where

00:59.360 --> 01:01.960
you are inserting data to the database.

01:02.000 --> 01:06.650
And another one, let's say that you are updating the content of your tables and so on.

01:06.650 --> 01:10.370
So you have like different statements in order to interact with the database.

01:10.370 --> 01:15.530
Now let's say that what you are doing is not only one time job, you are keep repeating those steps

01:15.570 --> 01:16.650
over and over.

01:16.650 --> 01:22.930
So you are always like doing an insert, then an update and then a select, and you keep repeating that

01:22.970 --> 01:23.970
day after day.

01:24.010 --> 01:29.290
So now imagine that you are doing something crazy where you go on vacation, but the job should be done.

01:29.290 --> 01:34.570
So what you do, you hand over all those select statements to your colleagues and they have to do it

01:34.570 --> 01:36.530
every day as well as you are gone.

01:36.530 --> 01:41.770
So you go and give them all those SQL scripts and you tell them, okay, you have to execute the first

01:41.770 --> 01:44.730
query, then the second query and then the third query.

01:44.730 --> 01:50.290
This is of course not a good way on how to do things, because of course there will be some human errors

01:50.290 --> 01:56.330
where like the execution of the script is not correct, like first updating, then inserting and things

01:56.330 --> 01:57.330
can go wrong.

01:57.370 --> 02:01.530
And that's exactly why we have stored procedures in SQL.

02:01.570 --> 02:08.750
So what we can do, we can put all those SQL statements together in one frame in one program and we

02:08.790 --> 02:10.430
call it stored procedure.

02:10.430 --> 02:15.630
And now once you do that, all your SQL statements will not stay at the client side.

02:15.790 --> 02:19.150
They will be stored now in the server side of the database.

02:19.150 --> 02:24.870
So that means in stored procedures we are storing our SQL statements inside the database.

02:24.870 --> 02:29.030
So you don't have to go and hand over your SQL statements to your colleagues.

02:29.070 --> 02:34.910
And now all what you have to do in order to interact with your SQL statements is to go and execute the

02:34.910 --> 02:35.910
stored procedure.

02:35.910 --> 02:40.470
So you write very simple command called execute SP, for example.

02:40.470 --> 02:44.990
So with that you are calling your stored procedure that is stored inside the server.

02:44.990 --> 02:47.430
And once you execute this what can happen.

02:47.430 --> 02:53.230
That database is going to go to the stored procedure and start executing all the SQL statements that

02:53.230 --> 02:55.390
you have inside the stored procedure.

02:55.430 --> 02:58.670
And it's going to do it exactly in the order that you have defined.

02:58.670 --> 03:00.270
So from top to bottom.

03:00.270 --> 03:05.710
So now once the database went through all your SQL statements, it's going to return back to the user

03:05.750 --> 03:08.070
the data that we have from the selects.

03:08.070 --> 03:12.840
And with that things are really easy and you can tell your colleagues, okay, just execute this stored

03:12.840 --> 03:16.080
procedure and the rest can be done from the database.

03:16.200 --> 03:21.520
So with that you minimize the human errors and you make sure that everything can be executed as you

03:21.520 --> 03:21.960
wish.

03:22.080 --> 03:24.480
And as well as you are back from your vacation.

03:24.520 --> 03:25.240
Things are easier.

03:25.240 --> 03:27.840
You have to just go and execute the stored procedure.

03:27.960 --> 03:30.120
So this is what we mean with stored procedure.

03:30.160 --> 03:36.360
You can store inside it multiple SQL statements in specific order, and you can save it inside the database.

03:36.360 --> 03:40.280
And each time you need your SQL statements you can go and simply execute them.

03:44.320 --> 03:49.800
So now let's have a quick comparison between a normal query, a normal SQL statements compared to a

03:49.840 --> 03:50.840
stored procedure.

03:51.040 --> 03:55.040
So a normal SQL query you have like select from where and so on.

03:55.240 --> 03:57.600
This is like one time transaction.

03:57.600 --> 04:01.720
You are asking the database for one thing and the database is answering.

04:01.840 --> 04:03.840
So it is like one time request.

04:03.840 --> 04:08.240
But now in the other hand in the stored procedures you have multiple SQL statements.

04:08.240 --> 04:13.780
And once you execute the stored procedure there will be many interactions with the database in one go.

04:13.780 --> 04:19.020
So that means you will have multiple transactions that is happening in your stored procedure.

04:19.020 --> 04:21.820
So an SQL query, it is like a simple request.

04:21.860 --> 04:23.900
You need one thing and you are getting it.

04:23.900 --> 04:28.100
But on the other hand in the stored procedure it is like a program.

04:28.100 --> 04:32.620
As you are writing a code in any programming languages, it is more than one request.

04:32.740 --> 04:34.060
It has a lot of stuff.

04:34.180 --> 04:40.020
Like for example, you can go and build looping logic where we go and iterate through something, or

04:40.020 --> 04:45.420
you can go and build a control flow where you have a logic like the flow statements.

04:45.420 --> 04:48.060
So they are like different paths in your code.

04:48.060 --> 04:54.540
And as well in programming we have like parameters and variables in order to make our code dynamic and

04:54.540 --> 04:55.260
flexible.

04:55.260 --> 05:01.020
And as well we can build error handling on our code in order to customize what can happen if there is

05:01.060 --> 05:01.860
like an issue.

05:01.860 --> 05:06.540
So the stored procedure, it is like having a code like for example in Python.

05:06.540 --> 05:12.300
So that means you can do more complicated stuff compared to a simple query where you have only like

05:12.300 --> 05:13.220
one request.

05:13.260 --> 05:18.870
So in the stored procedures you are doing, like programming and coding, and it is more advanced than

05:18.910 --> 05:20.510
only just having a query.

05:20.510 --> 05:25.630
So that means if you are working with stored procedures, things can get more complicated and advanced.

05:25.630 --> 05:31.030
But of course you will get a lot of flexibility and reusability compared to a simple query.

05:35.110 --> 05:38.270
So now there is like another alternative to stored procedures.

05:38.390 --> 05:44.150
Well, you can go and put all your SQL statements in a Python code and things can work as well.

05:44.150 --> 05:49.510
So either you put your SQL statements inside the stored procedure or in a Python code.

05:49.510 --> 05:52.390
But now the big question is what are the differences between them?

05:52.430 --> 05:57.070
Well, there is like a disadvantage if you are having Python in different server because you have to

05:57.070 --> 06:03.390
go and build a connection between your server and the database server and connection means always networking

06:03.390 --> 06:06.550
and you might get slightly worse performance.

06:06.550 --> 06:08.830
So this is one advantage for the stored procedure.

06:08.830 --> 06:13.950
Another advantage for search procedure that all the scripts that you are going to store inside the stored

06:13.950 --> 06:17.390
procedure in the database can be precompiled.

06:17.390 --> 06:17.410
Compiled.

06:17.410 --> 06:23.650
So precompiled means the SQL database servers knows already about your SQL statements, and there was

06:23.650 --> 06:29.690
already a check whether all your syntaxes are correct and the database as well can be preparing everything

06:29.730 --> 06:34.570
to execute the stored procedure, like maybe preparing the execution plans and a lot of stuff.

06:34.570 --> 06:39.650
So if you store your SQL statements inside stored procedure in the database, it is very close to the

06:39.650 --> 06:40.210
database.

06:40.210 --> 06:44.850
And the database knows everything about your scripts and it is ready to execute it.

06:44.850 --> 06:50.370
But if you put all your SQL statements outside of the database, of course the database has no chance

06:50.370 --> 06:56.970
to understand what is coming, so it cannot go and compile anything until Python sends the code to the

06:57.010 --> 06:57.850
database.

06:57.850 --> 07:00.890
So this is another advantage for the stored procedure.

07:00.890 --> 07:05.530
But now if you build your SQL statements in Python, you will get a lot of advantages.

07:05.570 --> 07:11.170
Like for example, you can go and build very flexible Python codes where you can use Python features

07:11.170 --> 07:12.610
together with the SQL.

07:12.650 --> 07:16.210
And with that you open the door of many possibilities and flexibility.

07:16.250 --> 07:20.130
Another thing with Python you can make great version control.

07:20.180 --> 07:22.820
So everything is integrated in Python tools.

07:22.820 --> 07:27.300
And one more advantage is that if you have a complex requirement in your projects, it's going to be

07:27.300 --> 07:29.860
really hard to implement it in stored procedures.

07:29.860 --> 07:34.500
It can cause you a lot of lines of code and things can be not comfortable.

07:34.500 --> 07:39.620
But if you are implementing a complex logic in Python, things can be way easier.

07:39.620 --> 07:45.140
So with Python you can implement complex logics very easily compared to the stored procedure.

07:45.140 --> 07:48.460
So those are the big differences between the stored procedure and Python.

07:48.460 --> 07:53.340
Now I have to be honest with you about having your code in stored procedure or in Python.

07:53.340 --> 07:58.300
Well, if you are working together in a data project, I will never recommend you to use stored procedure

07:58.300 --> 08:01.460
if you have the possibility to have your code in Python.

08:01.460 --> 08:07.300
And that's because I saw a lot of projects using stored procedure, and most of them ends in chaos.

08:07.340 --> 08:08.780
It is really hard to debug.

08:08.780 --> 08:10.220
It is really hard to test.

08:10.260 --> 08:11.580
It's like catastrophic.

08:11.580 --> 08:17.500
So really don't use in your projects any stored procedures, especially if you have like a big project

08:17.500 --> 08:20.060
and you have a lot of data and tables and so on.

08:20.220 --> 08:25.720
You can manage everything perfectly using Python, especially if you have a platform like Databricks

08:25.720 --> 08:27.000
or Snowflake's.

08:27.000 --> 08:31.360
Then of course, the best way to control your data projects is using Python.

08:31.360 --> 08:35.840
But of course, if you don't have this possibility and you have only a database server and you can only

08:35.840 --> 08:38.240
work with this, then you don't have any other option.

08:38.240 --> 08:40.080
You have to work with the stored procedures.

08:40.080 --> 08:45.160
But if you have this possibility to put your project inside Python and to run your scripts from there,

08:45.360 --> 08:47.960
then it is way better than having a stored procedure.

08:47.960 --> 08:49.040
Well, this is my opinion.

08:49.040 --> 08:52.560
I'm just talking about working in projects in big projects.

08:52.560 --> 08:56.880
But if you have like small projects, few tables and so on, then it's fine to stay with the stored

08:56.880 --> 09:03.200
procedure, but never build a big project using stored procedures because I tell you it will never work.

09:03.200 --> 09:08.560
So try to always to think about to have the right platform in order to run your projects.

09:08.600 --> 09:09.720
And now I'm thinking about it.

09:09.720 --> 09:13.560
Maybe I should have put this tip at the end of the video, not in the middle.

09:13.720 --> 09:14.680
So whatever.

09:14.680 --> 09:17.600
If you still want to learn stored procedures, we can continue on that.

09:17.600 --> 09:22.720
And I'm going to have like a really nice example about how to build stored procedures step by step,

09:22.720 --> 09:24.320
like having a mini projects.

09:24.320 --> 09:25.840
So why not learning both of them.

09:25.840 --> 09:26.480
So let's go.
